\details{
\subsection{Why this matters for case counts}{

Querying the inpatient pull with \code{HasBeenE = 0} added on purpose --
specifically to exclude anything already captured by the
\code{HasBeenE = 1} pull -- looks like a safe way to avoid double-counting.
By the API's own field semantics it should be: \code{HasBeenE = 0} means no
ED encounter appears anywhere in that record's history. It isn't. Some
records that correctly show \code{HasBeenE = 0} were, clinically, discharged
from the ED and immediately readmitted -- the ED encounter lives
entirely on a separate record already captured in the first pull, so
the exclusion filter never catches it. If you don't know this and want
to include inpatient admissions in your burden counts, summing the two
pulls double-counts that proportion of ED visits a second time. This is
the same problem \code{\link[=dedupe]{dedupe()}} solves for retransmitted or split ESSENCE
rows -- the duplicate here doesn't share a flag value that would mark
it as related. \code{link_encounters()} finds the relationship directly, by
checking for a matching ED record under the same \code{facility_col} x
\code{visit_col} key, instead of trusting \code{HasBeenE} alone.
}

\subsection{The care pathway artifact problem}{

A row showing \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1} and \code{HasBeenE = 0}
(\code{C_Patient_Class = "I"} or \code{"D"}) can mean either of two very different
things, and nothing in that single row tells you which:

\strong{The continuity-break artifact.} The patient \emph{was} triaged and treated
in the ED first, but the facility's system closed that encounter as a
discharge instead of tracking the ED-to-inpatient transition as one
continuous record. The result is two records sharing the same
\code{facility_col} x \code{visit_col} key -- one correctly showing \code{HasBeenE = 1},
one showing \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1} and \code{HasBeenE = 0} -- that otherwise
look unrelated. The ED record's fields (e.g. \code{Discharge_Disposition},
\code{CCDD}, \code{C_Death}) reflect only what was known at ED discharge, not the
outcome of the encounter that actually continued. Counting both records
separately double-counts a single real-world event. This is a data
quality artifact, not a distinct clinical pathway.

\strong{The invisibility gap.} A patient admitted directly to an inpatient
unit without ED triage -- via physician referral, a pre-arranged
admission, or similar -- genuinely has \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1} and
\code{HasBeenE = 0} with no preceding ED record to find. This visit never
appears in a \code{HasBeenE = 1} query regardless of which syndrome
definition or date range is used. This is a real clinical pathway, not
a data quality problem -- but a \code{HasBeenE = 1}-only pull will always
miss it.

The only way to tell these two cases apart is to check whether a
matching ED record exists under the same \code{facility_col} x \code{visit_col}
key. \code{link_encounters()} does exactly this: it links records sharing
that key (the continuity-break artifact's records already share it --
no cross-\code{Visit_ID} matching is needed), and, by default, merges each
episode's rows into one composite row so information from the
direct-admit record is reconciled onto the surviving row rather than
discarded.
}

\subsection{Merge behavior (\code{return_format = "collapsed"}, the default)}{

Every column matching \verb{has_been_*} is reconciled by taking the max across
the episode's rows -- e.g., if the ED row has \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 0} and the
direct-admit row has \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1}, the merged row correctly shows
\code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1}.

Columns named in \code{merge_fields} are reconciled using the strategy assigned
to them:
\describe{
\item{\code{"concat"}}{Starting from the primary row's value, appends each
other row's non-empty value if it is not already a substring of the
accumulated text (\code{"; "}-separated). Generic free text.}
\item{\code{"union_delimited"}}{Splits each row's value on \code{merge_delimiter},
takes the union of unique parts (preserving order of first
appearance), rejoins with the same delimiter.}
\item{\code{"union_ccdd"}}{Specific to ESSENCE's \code{CC-values|DD-values}
structure (\code{CCDD}/\code{CCDDParsed}). Splits on \code{|} into CC/DD halves
(fixed by the ESSENCE convention, not configurable), splits each half
on \code{merge_delimiter}, unions unique values within each half
separately, rejoins.}
\item{\code{"prefer_yes"}}{If any row's value is affirmative -- case-insensitive
\code{"Yes"}, or \code{1}/\code{"1"} for 0/1-coded flag columns -- the merged value is
that row's affirmative value. Otherwise falls back to the primary
row's value.}
\item{\code{"prefer_admission"}}{Uses the value from whichever row's
\code{patient_class} is \code{"Inpatient"}, \code{"Direct Admit"}, or \code{"Admitted"},
if such a row has a non-missing value for the field. Otherwise falls
back to the primary row's value.}
}

Any column not a \code{HasBeen_} flag and not listed in \code{merge_fields} takes
its value from the \strong{primary row}: the \code{"ED"}-class row if one exists in
the episode, else the first row in original order.

Set \code{return_format = "long"} to get the diagnostic long-format output
instead -- one row per patient-class per episode, with no merge applied.
Useful for inspecting the raw linkage mechanism directly.
}

\subsection{Why \code{inpatient_admission_data} is required}{

\code{link_encounters()} supplements the ED pull with a separately queried
inpatient pull (\code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1} or \code{HasBeenI = 1}). Rows with
\code{HasBeenE = 1} are automatically removed from the inpatient pull to
prevent duplicate rows for the same underlying record.

A \code{HasBeenE = 1} pull cannot resolve the care pathway artifact problem on
its own, in either direction: a genuine direct admission (\code{HasBeenE = 0})
is structurally absent from it by construction, and an ED-to-inpatient
escalation that \emph{is} visible within it already lives on one
already-deduplicated record (both \code{HasBeenE} and \code{HasBeenAdmitted} set),
which needs no linking -- there was never a second record to link
against. Supplying \code{inpatient_admission_data} is what makes linking
possible at all; \code{link_encounters()} aborts without it rather than
silently returning \code{ed_data} unchanged with cosmetic metadata columns
appended.

\strong{Do not row-bind \code{ed_data} and \code{inpatient_admission_data} into one data
frame and call \code{\link[=dedupe]{dedupe()}} on the combined result instead of using the
two-pull approach above.} \code{dedupe()}'s \code{keep} strategies are not aware
of the distinction between an ED record and its corresponding
direct-admit record -- both just look like two rows sharing a
\code{facility_col} x \code{visit_col} key -- and will discard one of them based
on \code{order_by} rather than merging them, silently and unpredictably
reducing either the ED or the direct-admit count depending on which
record's \code{Arrived_Date_Time} happens to win. Always deduplicate each
pull separately, then pass both to \code{link_encounters()}.
}

\subsection{Linking key and its limitation}{

Records are linked by \code{facility_col} \eqn{\times} \code{visit_col}
(\code{HospitalName} \eqn{\times} \code{Visit_ID} by default).

\strong{Limitation:} if a facility's HL7 feed assigns a genuinely different
\code{Visit_ID} to the inpatient leg of a care episode, \code{link_encounters()}
cannot detect the relationship -- the two records will appear as separate
episodes. This is a distinct scenario from the continuity-break artifact
above (which assumes the \code{Visit_ID} is shared) and is not addressed by
this function. If your data
includes \code{C_Unique_Patient_ID} (MRN), cross-referencing collapsed output
against a patient-level deduplication pass is a reasonable additional QA
step for this scenario.
}

\subsection{Patient class derivation: HasBeen_ pivot (standard)}{

\code{HasBeen_} flag columns (\code{HasBeenE}, \code{HasBeenAdmitted}/\code{HasBeenI},
\code{HasBeenO}) are convenience columns that ESSENCE derives from
\code{C_Patient_Class_List} -- they are easier to interpret and are the
fields most existing pulls and case definitions already include, so
\code{link_encounters()} uses them by default. They are pivoted to long
format, with each flag with value \code{1} contributing one row.
\code{HasBeenAdmitted} is preferred over \code{HasBeenI} when both are present.
}

\subsection{Patient class derivation: C_Patient_Class_List (optional, more granular)}{

\code{C_Patient_Class_List} is the underlying ESSENCE-computed field the
\code{HasBeen_} flags are themselves derived from: an alphabetic, deduplicated
list of all \code{C_Patient_Class} values present across messages sharing the
same ESSENCE ID (e.g., \code{"E"}, \code{"EI"}, \code{"EIO"}). When present, it is used
in place of the \code{HasBeen_} pivot, since it distinguishes patient classes
(e.g. Direct Admit vs. Inpatient, or Observation/Outpatient/Obstetrics/
Pre-admit/Recurring) that the \code{HasBeen_} flags do not represent. For more
granular and informative output, add \code{C_Patient_Class_List} to your
ESSENCE API pull fields. Splitting each character maps to the PHIN VADS
"Patient Class (Syndromic Surveillance)" value set (concept codes and
preferred names, verified against
\url{https://phinvads.cdc.gov/vads/ViewValueSet.action?id=564F8F8B-E1DE-E411-8970-0017A477041A}).
\code{link_encounters()}'s own derived \code{patient_class} output values (\code{"ED"},
\code{"Inpatient"}, \code{"Direct Admit"}, etc.) are shortened working labels, not
required to match these preferred names verbatim:\tabular{ll}{
   Code \tab PHIN VADS preferred name \cr
   \code{D} \tab Direct admit \cr
   \code{E} \tab Emergency \cr
   \code{I} \tab Inpatient \cr
   \code{V} \tab Observation patient \cr
   \code{B} \tab Obstetrics \cr
   \code{O} \tab Outpatient \cr
   \code{P} \tab Preadmit \cr
   \code{R} \tab Recurring patient \cr
}

}

\subsection{Chronological ordering of \code{.patient_class_sequence}}{

\code{.patient_class_sequence} reflects the actual order encounters occurred
in, not alphabetical order -- e.g. \code{"Direct Admit->ED"} when the
direct-admit record's timestamp precedes the ED record's, which is the
reverse of what typically indicates the continuity-break artifact
(an ED visit that transitions into a direct-admit readmission, not a
direct admit that precedes an ED visit). Ordering uses the first
available field, per row, in this priority:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{\code{C_Patient_Class_MDT_Updates}} (requires \code{C_Patient_Class_List}).
A concatenated list of timestamps positionally aligned with
\code{C_Patient_Class_List}, giving the exact moment each class was
assigned -- the only field that can order two classes assigned
within a single record (e.g. \code{"EI"}). Rows where the two lists'
lengths disagree fall back to the next tier.
\item \strong{\code{C_Visit_Date_Time}}. Applied per record -- all classes
derived from one record share that record's timestamp, so this only
differentiates classes across separate records sharing the same
\code{facility_col} x \code{visit_col} key (i.e. the ED record vs. the
direct-admit record in the continuity-break artifact, or in the
two-pull approach).
\item \strong{\code{Date} + \code{Time}} (both required). Combined into a timestamp
when neither field above is present.
}
If none of these fields are present (or none can be parsed) anywhere in
the data, \code{link_encounters()} warns once and \code{.patient_class_sequence}
falls back to alphabetical order for every episode. Within a single
episode, if only some classes have a usable timestamp, timed classes are
ordered first and untimed classes are appended last.
}

\subsection{Episode metadata columns}{

Present regardless of \code{return_format}. In collapsed output, these
describe the episode the collapsed row was built from (e.g.
\code{.episode_n_rows = 2} on a collapsed row means two original records were
merged into it).
\describe{
\item{\code{.episode_id}}{A character key combining \code{facility_col} and
\code{visit_col}, shared across all rows belonging to the same care episode.}
\item{\code{.patient_class_sequence}}{All patient classes for the episode
in chronological order and collapsed, e.g., \code{"Direct Admit->ED"}
when the direct admit occurred first -- see Details.}
\item{\code{.episode_n_rows}}{Number of original rows the episode was built
from before merging.}
\item{\code{.index_encounter}}{In long format, \code{TRUE} on the row that
survives filtering to one row per episode. In collapsed format, always
\code{TRUE} (retained for schema consistency with long format).}
}
}
}
\examples{
# essence_ed_raw and essence_inp_raw are two small synthetic datasets
# representing the two separately queried ESSENCE pulls link_encounters()
# expects -- see `?essence_ed_raw`/`?essence_inp_raw`
ed_clean <- essence_ed_raw |>
  dedupe(order_by = Arrived_Date_Time, keep = "last") |>
  filter_care_setting(
    fix_facility_type_vector = c("Hillside FSED", "Downtown Emergency Services")
  )
inpatient_clean <- dedupe(
  essence_inp_raw, order_by = Arrived_Date_Time, keep = "last"
)

# One row per true encounter, merged
episodes <- link_encounters(ed_clean, inpatient_clean)
nrow(episodes)

# Inspect the distribution of care pathways
episodes |>
  dplyr::count(.patient_class_sequence, sort = TRUE)

# Long format: inspect the raw linkage mechanism directly
episodes_long <- link_encounters(
  ed_clean, inpatient_clean, return_format = "long"
)

\dontrun{
# Real usage: two separate ESSENCE queries, each deduplicated on its own
ed_clean        <- essence_ed        |> dedupe(order_by = Arrived_Date_Time)
inpatient_clean <- essence_inpatient |> dedupe(order_by = Arrived_Date_Time)

episodes_full <- link_encounters(
  ed_clean, inpatient_clean,
  merge_fields = c(TriageNotes = "concat")
)
}

}
